<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tunjur kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1650 CE (approx.) · capital Ain Farah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mysterious kingdom that ruled Darfur and Wadai before the Keira sultans, remembered through traditions and the ruins of Ain Farah.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The origins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunjur">Tunjur</a> are obscure and debated: traditions variously claim Arab, Nubian or local descent. By around 1400 they had replaced the earlier <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/daju-kingdom">Daju kingdom</a> as the dominant power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrah-mountains">Jebel Marra</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> region, and their rule extended west into what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Almost nothing is documented about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunjur">Tunjur</a> government. The red-brick ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-farah">Ain Farah</a>, including what may be a mosque and earlier church-like structures, are attributed to them, suggesting links with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. They are traditionally credited with introducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> and controlling caravan trade between the Nile and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> basin.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunjur">Tunjur</a> rule was overthrown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-karim-ibn-jama">Abd al-Karim</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keira-dynasty">Keira dynasty</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a>. The Tunjur survived as a people in Darfur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1400 CE – 1650 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.56%;width:4.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1650 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ain Farah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a href="/countries/chad">Chad</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tunjur, Tunjur dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1525&amp;focus=tunjur-kingdom" aria-label="Tunjur kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5345 1647 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tunjur kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5665 1903L5695 1926M5665 1903L5571 1919"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5695 1926h0M5571 1919h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5665 1903h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5665 1903h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ain Farah. <a href="/atlas?year=1525&amp;focus=tunjur-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
